    {
      "id": "p0130-b010",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "source": {
        "text": "This multi-layered line of dialogue would be flattened in a literal translation into Japanese, so the translator replaced the original reference with the Japanese phrase teishu genki de inu ga ii (\"it’s good to have a husband who is healthy and a dog\"). This new phrase is a reference to a Japanese manga series of the same name and is a play on the idiom teishu genki de rusu ga ii (\"it’s good to have a husband who is healthy and away\").",
        "raw": "This multi-layered line of dialogue would be flattened in a literal translation\ninto Japanese, so the translator replaced the original reference with the\n.Japanese phrase teislm genki de inu ga ii (\"it's good to have a husband who is\nhealthy and a dog\"). This new phrase is a reference to a .Japanese manga series\nof the same name and is a play on the idiom teishu genki de rusu ga ii (\"it's good\nto have a husband who is healthy and away\").",
        "status": "corrected",
        "languages": [
          "en"
        ],
        "bbox": [
